🚀 Today’s Topic: async let vs TaskGroup (Deep Dive)

This is a very common senior iOS interview + real-world design topic.

🧠 1️⃣ async let — Fixed Parallel Work

Use when:

Number of tasks is known at compile time
Tasks are independent
You want clean + minimal syntax
Example
func fetchDashboard() async throws -> (User, Posts) {
    async let user = fetchUser()
    async let posts = fetchPosts()

    return try await (user, posts)
}
✅ Characteristics
Structured concurrency
Automatically awaited before scope exits
Errors propagate immediately
Lightweight and fast

👉 Think:

“I know exactly how many calls I need”

🧠 2️⃣ withTaskGroup — Dynamic Parallel Work

Use when:

Number of tasks is dynamic
You need loop-based concurrency
You want fine-grained control
Example
func fetchPosts(ids: [Int]) async -> [Post] {
    await withTaskGroup(of: Post?.self) { group in
        for id in ids {
            group.addTask {
                return try? await fetchPost(id: id)
            }
        }

        var results: [Post] = []

        for await post in group {
            if let post = post {
                results.append(post)
            }
        }

        return results
    }
}
🔥 Key Differences (Interview Gold)
Feature	async let	TaskGroup
Task count	Fixed	Dynamic
Syntax	Simple	Verbose
Error handling	Automatic	Manual control
Cancellation	Automatic	Manual control possible
Use case	2–5 API calls	Lists, loops, batching
⚠️ Subtle Behavior (Senior-Level Insight)
async let → Implicit Await

This:

async let a = fetchA()
async let b = fetchB()

👉 MUST be awaited before function exits
Otherwise Swift throws a runtime error.

TaskGroup → Manual Lifecycle

You control:

When tasks are added
How results are consumed
Whether to ignore failures
🧠 3️⃣ Error Handling Difference
async let
async let a = try fetchA()
async let b = try fetchB()

try await (a, b)

👉 If one fails → everything fails immediately

TaskGroup (Flexible)
group.addTask {
    try? await fetchA() // ignore failure
}

👉 You can:

Ignore errors
Retry
Partially succeed
🧠 4️⃣ Cancellation Behavior
async let → cancels siblings automatically on failure
TaskGroup → you can manually cancel:
group.cancelAll()
🎯 Real-World Mapping
Use async let for:
Dashboard APIs
Parallel independent calls
Small fixed sets
Use TaskGroup for:
Fetching list of items
Image downloads
Batch processing
Retry systems
🧪 Interview Question (Try This)

👉 What happens here?

func test() async throws {
    async let a = fetchA()
    async let b = fetchB()

    print("Done")
}
Answer:

❌ Runtime error — because a and b are never awaited

💡 Pro Tip (Production Insight)

If you’re building networking layers (like your Android/iOS parity work):

👉 Prefer:

async let → for endpoint fan-out
TaskGroup → for collections / pagination
🏁 Today’s Takeaway

Use async let for simplicity and performance when tasks are fixed.
Use TaskGroup when you need scalability and control.
